---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Arrow from '../components/Arrow.astro';
import SourceLink from '../components/SourceLink.astro';
import ExchangeDiagram from '../components/ExchangeDiagram.astro';
import { science, type ScienceSourceKey } from '../data/science';
import '../styles/science.css';
const orbit = Array.from({ length: 18 }, (_, i) => {
  const angle = i / 18 * Math.PI * 2 - Math.PI / 2;
  return { x: 220 + Math.cos(angle) * (i % 3 === 0 ? 170 : 145), y: 210 + Math.sin(angle) * (i % 3 === 0 ? 170 : 145) };
});
---
<Base title={science.title} description={science.description}>
  <section class="science-hero" aria-labelledby="page-title">
    <div class="science-hero-inner wrap">
      <div><p class="section-index">Matching theory & market design</p><h1 id="page-title">{science.headline.map(line => <span class="line">{line}</span>)}</h1><p class="lead">{science.intro}</p><a class="text-link" href="#the-idea">Start with the idea<Arrow direction="down" /></a></div>
      <figure class="science-orbit" aria-label="Illustrative network of possible relationships and selected matches">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 440 420" aria-hidden="true">
          <circle cx="220" cy="210" r="190" fill="none" stroke="#676080" stroke-dasharray="2 10" />
          <g fill="none" stroke="#8b81b2" stroke-opacity=".4">{orbit.map((point, i) => [5,7].map(step => <path d={`M${point.x} ${point.y}Q220 210 ${orbit[(i+step)%18].x} ${orbit[(i+step)%18].y}`} />))}</g>
          <g fill="none" stroke="#00f59b" stroke-width="1.5">{orbit.filter((_,i) => i%2===0).map((point,i) => <path d={`M${point.x} ${point.y}Q220 210 ${orbit[i*2+1].x} ${orbit[i*2+1].y}`} />)}</g>
          {orbit.map((point,i) => <circle cx={point.x} cy={point.y} r={i%3===0?5:3.5} fill={i%2===0?'#00f59b':'#d6cfee'} />)}
          <circle cx="220" cy="210" r="34" fill="#211b3b" stroke="#9b8ebf" /><path d="m208 210 8 8 16-18" fill="none" stroke="#00f59b" stroke-width="2" />
        </svg>
        <figcaption>Preferences. Rules. Possibilities.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <nav class="science-contents wrap" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#the-idea">The idea</a><a href="#in-practice">In practice</a><a href="/approach/">Our approach<Arrow /></a></nav>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap science-intro" id="the-idea" aria-labelledby="idea-title">
    <div class="section-heading"><h2 id="idea-title">Every allocation<br />has a design.</h2><div><p>When requests compete, someone must decide how to allocate what is available. A queue, a lottery, a preference list and an auction each answer that question differently.</p><p>The research asks what those rules do: to participation, incentives, access and the outcomes people receive. <SourceLink source="nobel" /></p></div></div>
    <ol class="science-concepts">{science.concepts.map(item => <li><span class="section-index">{item.number}</span><h3>{item.title}</h3><p class="concept-question">{item.question}</p><p>{item.body}</p></li>)}</ol>
    <p class="science-note">Stability, efficiency and fairness are different criteria. A good design makes the objectives—and the trade-offs—explicit.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="nobel-section" id="nobel" aria-labelledby="nobel-title">
    <div class="nobel-inner wrap"><div class="nobel-year" aria-hidden="true">2012<span>The Nobel recognition</span></div><div><p class="section-index">From mathematical ideas to working institutions</p><h2 id="nobel-title">A scientific foundation.<br />A practical discipline.</h2><p class="lead">The 2012 Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences was awarded to Alvin E. Roth and Lloyd S. Shapley for work on stable allocations and the practice of market design. <SourceLink source="nobel" /></p><p>The field’s practical advances also draw on a wider research community. School-choice work by Atila Abdulkadiroğlu and Parag Pathak, and kidney-exchange work by Roth, Tayfun Sönmez and M. Utku Ünver, illustrate that shared foundation. <SourceLink source="roth" /></p></div></div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap" id="in-practice" aria-labelledby="practice-title">
    <div class="section-heading"><h2 id="practice-title">Research, in the<br />decisions around us.</h2><p>These ideas are used where people’s choices meet limited capacity, eligibility rules and the choices of others.</p></div>
    <ol class="science-applications">{science.applications.map(item => <li><span class="method-number">{item.number}</span><div><h3>{item.title}</h3><p class="application-subject">{item.subject}</p></div><p>{item.body} <SourceLink source={item.source as ScienceSourceKey} /></p></li>)}</ol>
  </section>

  <section class="case-study-section" id="organ-exchange" aria-labelledby="exchange-case-title">
    <div class="wrap case-study-heading"><p class="section-index">Research in practice / Organ exchange</p><h2 id="exchange-case-title">A match can change<br />what is possible.</h2><p class="lead">Considering a pool of donor–patient pairs can reveal possibilities that separate decisions miss.</p></div>
    <div class="wrap case-study-layout"><div class="case-study-prose"><p>At İnönü University in Malatya, the Banu Bedestenci Sönmez Liver Exchange Program applies this idea to living-donor transplantation. A team led by economists Tayfun Sönmez and M. Utku Ünver developed the matching system in collaboration with Sezai Yılmaz and the clinical team. <SourceLink source="bc" /></p><p>The program enabled the first four-way liver exchange in 2022. In June 2026, the university reported an eight-way exchange. <SourceLink source="bc" /> <SourceLink source="inonu" /></p><p>Turning the model into practice means designing around compatibility, ethical requirements and operational constraints—and coordinating the exchange as a whole.</p></div><ExchangeDiagram /></div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap science-reading" aria-label="Sources and context"><details><summary>Sources & context<span>{Object.keys(science.sources).length} primary references</span></summary><p class="science-note">Sources for the examples above. Reviewed 27 October 2026.</p><ol>{Object.values(science.sources).map(item => <li><span>{item.number}</span><a href={item.href}><strong>{item.name}</strong><span>{item.title}</span><Arrow direction="up-right" /></a></li>)}</ol></details></section>

  <section class="science-return"><div class="wrap"><p class="section-index">At Match & Design</p><h2>From rules<br />to working software.</h2><p>We connect market design, optimization and software. Eduscope brings this approach to academic planning and allocation.</p><div class="science-return-links"><a class="text-link" href="/approach/">Explore our approach<Arrow /></a><a class="text-link" href="/work/">Discover Eduscope<Arrow /></a></div></div></section>
</Base>
